@* Which parts of this API does nothing use.

   That is the question this page exists to answer, and everything on it is
   arranged around the fact that the answer is an ABSENCE. An absence has no
   row of its own to read: nothing renders for the operation nobody calls, so a
   view that lists what is called reports the good news and leaves the reader
   to notice the silence. So the resource list is sorted by coverage ASCENDING,
   the state tally always shows the not-called count even when it is zero, the
   calls that fail to resolve get a callout rather than a filter, and the
   uncatalogued section is here at all.

   The mapping is somebody else's claim about this corpus. The page says where
   it was fetched from before it says anything the document claims, for the
   same reason the nav bar shows the catalogue's origin: a document can write
   any word inside itself, and the address it came from is the one thing about
   it its author does not get to choose. *@

@using System.Text
@using System.Text.RegularExpressions
@inject CoverageStore Coverage

<div class="page">
    <h1 class="page__title">Coverage</h1>

    @if (Coverage.Source is { } from)
    {
        <div class="cluster cluster--titled">
            <span class="cov-provenance__label">Mapping fetched from</span>
            <KeelChip class="cov-provenance" Mono="true" Emphasis="Emphasis.Quiet"
                      title="@($"Every figure on this page comes from the document served at {from}. Nothing here is read from what that document says about itself.")">@from</KeelChip>
        </div>
    }

    @if (Coverage.Refusal is { } refusal)
    {
        @* Verbatim, and on its own. The refusal already explains itself at
           length, and the whole point of it is that nothing behind it renders:
           partial coverage over a refusal is the failure the refusal exists to
           stop, wearing a warning label. *@
        <KeelCallout Tone="Tone.Bad" Live="LiveRegion.Off" Title="Coverage is not shown">
            @refusal
        </KeelCallout>
    }
    else if (Map is null)
    {
        <KeelCallout Tone="Tone.Neutral" Live="LiveRegion.Off" Title="No coverage mapping">
            Nothing has published a mapping for this catalogue, so there is no claim
            here about what calls these operations. A mapping can be pointed at with
            <code>?coverage=</code>, or named by the catalogue itself.
        </KeelCallout>
    }
    else if (_view is not { } view)
    {
        <div class="state">
            <KeelSpinner class="state__spinner" Diameter="22" Label="Reading the coverage mapping" />
            Reading the coverage mapping…
        </div>
    }
    else
    {
        <section class="section cov-headline">
            <KeelProgressBar class="cov-headline__bar" Value="@Percent(view.Recorded, view.Total)"
                             aria-label="@($"{view.Recorded} of {view.Total} operations have a caller recorded in this mapping")" />

            <p class="cov-headline__line">
                <strong>@view.Recorded</strong> of <strong>@view.Total</strong>
                operation@(view.Total == 1 ? "" : "s")
                @(view.Recorded == 1 ? "has" : "have") a caller recorded in this mapping,
                <strong>@view.Artifacts</strong> @(Kinds(view.Artifacts) + " in all.")
            </p>

            @* Every count, including the zeroes: a state with no members that
               is simply omitted is indistinguishable from a state this view
               does not know about, and the reader cannot tell which they are
               looking at.

               No tones. These are not states an operation entered; they are
               facts about it relative to one mapping, and the words already
               tell them apart in greyscale.

               And the third is deliberately NOT in the same row as the other
               two. Full and conditional are two points on one scale; an
               absence of any record is not a third point further along it, and
               three chips in a row would assert that it was. So the scale is a
               row and the absence is a sentence, which is also the only shape
               with room to say whose record it is an absence from. *@
            <div class="cluster cluster--tight">
                <KeelChip Emphasis="Emphasis.Outlined">@view.Full called in full</KeelChip>
                <KeelChip Emphasis="Emphasis.Outlined">@view.Conditional called conditionally</KeelChip>
            </div>

            <p class="cov-absence">
                <KeelChip Emphasis="Emphasis.Outlined">@view.Unrecorded with no caller recorded in this mapping</KeelChip>
                <span class="cov-absence__gloss">
                    That is a fact about this one mapping, published by one consumer of this
                    API. An operation with no row here may still have callers nobody has
                    mapped, so it is short of evidence rather than known to be unused.
                </span>
            </p>

            @* What the claims rest on, in the mapping's own declared words. A
               page of counts with no provenance on the counts reads as
               measurement; most mappings are read out of source rather than off
               the wire, and the difference is the reader's to weigh. *@
            @foreach (var grade in view.Grades)
            {
                <p class="cov-grade">
                    <strong>@grade.Calls</strong> of the @view.Calls calls shown here
                    @(grade.Calls == 1 ? "is" : "are") graded
                    <strong>@(grade.Title ?? grade.Id)</strong>@Caveat(grade)
                </p>
            }
            @if (view.ObservedTitle is { } observed)
            {
                <p class="cov-grade cov-grade--absent">
                    Nothing here is graded <strong>@observed</strong>, the grade this mapping
                    declares as its attested one.
                </p>
            }
        </section>

        <KeelSeparator />

        <section class="section">
            <h2 class="section__title">Resources, least covered first</h2>
            <p class="prop__desc">
                Sorted by the proportion of each resource this mapping records a caller
                for, ascending, so a resource with nothing recorded against it at all is at
                the top. Open one for its operations and, for each that is called, who
                calls it.
            </p>

            @foreach (var group in view.Groups)
            {
                <KeelDisclosure class="cov-group" Label="@group.Name">
                    <Trailing>
                        <KeelProgressBar class="cov-meter" Value="@Percent(group.Called, group.Rows.Count)"
                                         aria-label="@($"{group.Called} of {group.Rows.Count} operations have a caller recorded in this mapping")" />
                        <span class="cov-meter__count">@group.Called of @group.Rows.Count</span>
                    </Trailing>
                    <ChildContent>
                        @foreach (var row in group.Rows)
                        {
                            <div class="cov-op @(row.State == OpState.Unrecorded ? "cov-op--unrecorded" : "cov-op--called")">
                                @* Shape and words, never colour alone: a filled,
                                   half-filled and hollow ring are one glyph in
                                   three weights, which says "ordered, same
                                   thing" the way three unrelated marks would
                                   not, and the label beside it says the same in
                                   text for a reader who cannot see either. *@
                                @switch (row.State)
                                {
                                    case OpState.Full:
                                        <svg class="cov-glyph" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true">
                                            <circle cx="8" cy="8" r="6" fill="currentColor" />
                                        </svg>
                                        break;
                                    case OpState.Conditional:
                                        <svg class="cov-glyph" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true">
                                            <circle cx="8" cy="8" r="6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" />
                                            <path d="M8 2a6 6 0 0 0 0 12z" fill="currentColor" />
                                        </svg>
                                        break;
                                    default:
                                        <svg class="cov-glyph" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true">
                                            <circle cx="8" cy="8" r="6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" />
                                        </svg>
                                        break;
                                }

                                <div class="cov-op__body">
                                    <a class="cov-op__link" href="@OperationHash(row.Op)"
                                       @onclick:preventDefault @onclick="() => Go(row.Op)">
                                        <KeelChip Emphasis="Emphasis.Quiet" Mono="true"
                                                  Tone="@MethodTone.For(row.Op.Method)">@row.Op.Method</KeelChip>
                                        <span class="cov-op__id">@row.Op.OperationId</span>
                                    </a>
                                    <span class="cov-op__path">@row.Op.Path</span>
                                    <span class="cov-op__state">@Words(row.State)</span>

                                    @if (row.Callers.Count > 0)
                                    {
                                        @* Grouped by artifact, with the
                                           entrypoints under it. The producer's
                                           row is (artifact, entrypoint,
                                           operation), so a flat list counts one
                                           component five times for reading an
                                           operation from five lifecycle phases;
                                           the number of components is the fact,
                                           and which phases is the detail. *@
                                        <KeelDisclosure class="cov-callers"
                                                        Label="@($"{row.Callers.Count} {Kinds(row.Callers.Count)}")">
                                            @foreach (var caller in row.Callers)
                                            {
                                                <div class="cov-caller">
                                                    <div class="cov-caller__head">
                                                        <span class="cov-caller__name">@Named(caller.Item)</span>
                                                        @if (KindLabel(caller.Item.Kind) is { } kindLabel)
                                                        {
                                                            <KeelTag>@kindLabel</KeelTag>
                                                        }
                                                    </div>
                                                    @foreach (var call in caller.Calls)
                                                    {
                                                        <div class="cov-entry">
                                                            <span class="cov-entry__where">@EntrypointLabel(call)</span>
                                                            <span class="cov-entry__how">@(call.Coverage == "partial" ? "conditionally" : "in full")</span>
                                                            @if (call.ApiVersion is { Length: > 0 } apiVersion)
                                                            {
                                                                <KeelChip Emphasis="Emphasis.Quiet" Mono="true"
                                                                          title="The api-version this caller pins">@apiVersion</KeelChip>
                                                            }
                                                            @if (call.Approximate == true)
                                                            {
                                                                <KeelTag Tone="Tone.Warning"
                                                                         title="The producer could not resolve this row with certainty">approximate</KeelTag>
                                                            }
                                                        </div>
                                                        @if (call.Note is { Length: > 0 } note)
                                                        {
                                                            <p class="cov-entry__note">@note</p>
                                                        }
                                                    }
                                                    @if (caller.Item.Source?.Path is { Length: > 0 } path)
                                                    {
                                                        <p class="cov-caller__source">@path@(caller.Item.Source?.Line is { } line ? $":{line}" : "")</p>
                                                    }
                                                </div>
                                            }
                                        </KeelDisclosure>
                                    }
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        }
                    </ChildContent>
                </KeelDisclosure>
            }
        </section>

        @if (view.Misses.Count > 0)
        {
            @* A finding, not an error and not a thing to hide. Every one of
               these is a call the mapping makes that this spec cannot account
               for, so the coverage above is measured against a slightly smaller
               claim than the mapping actually made, and a reader who cannot see
               that will read the numbers as complete. *@
            <section class="section">
                <KeelCallout Tone="Tone.Warning" Live="LiveRegion.Off" Title="@MissTitle(view.MissedCalls)">
                    <p class="cov-miss__lead">
                        These are counted in nothing above. Each one names an operation and
                        this spec has none to match it, so the mapping and the spec disagree
                        about what exists.
                    </p>
                    <ul class="cov-miss">
                        @foreach (var miss in view.Misses)
                        {
                            <li>
                                <span class="cov-miss__wanted">@miss.Wanted</span>
                                <span class="cov-miss__why">@miss.Why</span>
                                <span class="cov-miss__who">
                                    @miss.Calls call@(miss.Calls == 1 ? "" : "s") from @string.Join(", ", miss.Artifacts)
                                </span>
                            </li>
                        }
                    </ul>
                </KeelCallout>
            </section>
        }

        @if (view.Uncatalogued.Count > 0)
        {
            @* Calls no operation names. Not scoped to this spec, and they
               cannot be: a call with no operation has no spec either, which is
               what "uncatalogued" means. They are on every spec's coverage page
               for that reason, and the section says so rather than letting a
               reader take them for this spec's. *@
            <section class="section">
                <h2 class="section__title">Calls no operation names</h2>
                <p class="prop__desc">
                    @view.UncataloguedCalls call@(view.UncataloguedCalls == 1 ? "" : "s")
                    from @view.UncataloguedArtifacts @Kinds(view.UncataloguedArtifacts)
                    that no operation in this catalogue can name, so they are attributed to
                    no spec and appear on every spec's coverage page. They are counted in
                    nothing above; without them the figures would read as a fuller account
                    of this code than the mapping claims to be.
                </p>

                @foreach (var reason in view.Uncatalogued)
                {
                    <h3 class="section__label">
                        @reason.Label
                        <span class="prop__meta">@reason.Calls call@(reason.Calls == 1 ? "" : "s")</span>
                    </h3>
                    @foreach (var row in reason.Rows)
                    {
                        <div class="cov-uncat">
                            <span class="cov-caller__name">@row.Artifact</span>
                            <span class="cov-uncat__count">@row.Count call@(row.Count == 1 ? "" : "s")</span>
                            @if (row.Note is { Length: > 0 } note)
                            {
                                <p class="cov-entry__note">@note</p>
                            }
                        </div>
                    }
                }
            </section>
        }
    }
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public OpenApiSpec Spec { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>
    /// The catalogue id of the spec on screen. Not derivable from the spec
    /// itself: a mapping's <c>spec</c> field is a catalogue id, and the
    /// document's own title is a different string chosen by a different person.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string SpecId { get; set; } = "";

    [Parameter] public EventCallback<Route> OnNavigate { get; set; }

    private CoverageMap? Map => Coverage.Map;

    private View? _view;

    // The store hands back the same task to every caller, so awaiting it here
    // is correct whether the shell got there first or not.
    protected override Task OnInitializedAsync() => Coverage.LoadAsync();

    // Runs after OnInitializedAsync has completed, so the map is present by the
    // time the join is built. Built once per parameter set rather than per
    // render: the join walks every operation in the spec against every called
    // identity, and a render is not the place for that.
    protected override void OnParametersSet() => _view = Map is { } map ? Build(map) : null;

    // ---- the model this page renders --------------------------------------

    /// <summary>
    /// What this mapping records about one operation.
    ///
    /// <para>
    /// <b>Three members, but not three points on one scale.</b> Full and
    /// Conditional are positive claims with a call site behind them, ordered
    /// against each other. <see cref="OpState.Unrecorded"/> is not further down
    /// that scale, it is the absence of a row in ONE mapping published by ONE
    /// consumer of this API, and the page has to keep those apart in its words
    /// and in its layout. A call to an operation this spec does not carry is a
    /// fourth thing again, an anomaly rather than a state, which is why it is
    /// not in here and gets a callout of its own.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private enum OpState { Unrecorded, Conditional, Full }

    private sealed record Caller(CoverageItem Item, IReadOnlyList<CoverageCall> Calls);

    private sealed record OpRow(Operation Op, OpState State, IReadOnlyList<Caller> Callers);

    private sealed record Group(string Name, int Called, IReadOnlyList<OpRow> Rows);

    private sealed record Miss(string Wanted, string Why, IReadOnlyList<string> Artifacts, int Calls);

    private sealed record GradeTally(string Id, string? Title, string? Caveat, int Calls);

    private sealed record UncatRow(string Artifact, int Count, string? Note);

    private sealed record UncatReason(string Label, int Calls, IReadOnlyList<UncatRow> Rows);

    private sealed record View(
        int Total, int Recorded, int Full, int Conditional, int Artifacts, int Calls,
        IReadOnlyList<Group> Groups,
        IReadOnlyList<Miss> Misses, int MissedCalls,
        IReadOnlyList<GradeTally> Grades, string? ObservedTitle,
        IReadOnlyList<UncatReason> Uncatalogued, int UncataloguedCalls, int UncataloguedArtifacts)
    {
        public int Unrecorded => Total - Recorded;
    }

    // ---- building it -------------------------------------------------------

    private View Build(CoverageMap map)
    {
        var byId = new Dictionary<string, List<Operation>>(StringComparer.Ordinal);
        foreach (var op in Spec.Operations)
        {
            if (!byId.TryGetValue(op.OperationId, out var same)) byId[op.OperationId] = same = new();
            same.Add(op);
        }

        var citations = new Dictionary<Operation, List<CoverageCitation>>();
        var misses = new List<Miss>();

        foreach (var identity in map.CalledOperations(SpecId))
        {
            var claims = map.Callers(SpecId, identity);
            var (op, why) = Resolve(identity, byId);

            if (op is null)
            {
                misses.Add(new Miss(
                    identity.ToString(),
                    why ?? "it does not resolve",
                    claims.Select(c => Named(c.Item)).Distinct(StringComparer.Ordinal).OrderBy(n => n, StringComparer.Ordinal).ToList(),
                    claims.Count));
                continue;
            }

            // Two identities can land on one operation: the same operation
            // named by id in one artifact and by path in another is one
            // operation with two sets of callers, and counting it twice would
            // put the called total above the spec's own.
            if (!citations.TryGetValue(op, out var all)) citations[op] = all = new();
            all.AddRange(claims);
        }

        // Calls with no identity at all. CoverageMap indexes by identity, so
        // these are invisible to every query on it; they are still calls this
        // mapping makes against this spec, and silently dropping them is the
        // failure this page is arranged against.
        var identityless = map.Items
            .SelectMany(item => item.Calls.Select(call => (item, call)))
            .Where(x => string.Equals(x.call.Spec, SpecId, StringComparison.Ordinal) && x.call.Identity is null)
            .ToList();

        if (identityless.Count > 0)
            misses.Add(new Miss(
                "no operation named",
                "the call names its operation in neither form, or in both, so there is nothing to look up",
                identityless.Select(x => Named(x.item)).Distinct(StringComparer.Ordinal).OrderBy(n => n, StringComparer.Ordinal).ToList(),
                identityless.Count));

        var rows = Spec.Operations.ToDictionary(
            op => op,
            op => citations.TryGetValue(op, out var claims)
                ? new OpRow(op, claims.Any(c => c.Call.Coverage != "partial") ? OpState.Full : OpState.Conditional, ByArtifact(claims))
                : new OpRow(op, OpState.Unrecorded, Array.Empty<Caller>()));

        // Grouped by primary tag, in the spec's declared tag order, exactly as
        // the nav tree and the resource page group it. One grouping in this app
        // or the coverage of a resource would be measured over a set the reader
        // cannot see anywhere else.
        var byTag = Spec.Operations
            .GroupBy(o => o.PrimaryTag, StringComparer.Ordinal)
            .ToDictionary(g => g.Key, g => g.ToList(), StringComparer.Ordinal);

        var groups = Spec.TagsInOrder()
            .Where(t => byTag.ContainsKey(t.Name))
            .Select(t => t.Name)
            .Distinct(StringComparer.Ordinal)
            .Select(name =>
            {
                var members = byTag[name].Select(op => rows[op]).ToList();
                return new Group(name, members.Count(r => r.State != OpState.Unrecorded), members);
            })
            // ASCENDING, and this is the whole design of the page. The reader's
            // first question is which parts of the API nothing uses, and an
            // ascending sort answers it with the ORDER of the list rather than
            // asking them to read every row and notice the small numbers. A
            // resource nothing touches is at the top before anything is read.
            // The tie-break is size descending, because among resources that
            // are equally untouched the biggest one is the biggest finding.
            .OrderBy(g => (double)g.Called / g.Rows.Count)
            .ThenByDescending(g => g.Rows.Count)
            .ThenBy(g => g.Name, StringComparer.Ordinal)
            .ToList();

        var shown = citations.Values.SelectMany(c => c).ToList();

        var grades = shown
            .GroupBy(c => c.Call.Grade ?? "", StringComparer.Ordinal)
            .OrderBy(g => map.RankOf(g.Key) ?? int.MaxValue)
            .Select(g => new GradeTally(g.Key, map.Grade(g.Key)?.Title, map.Grade(g.Key)?.Caveat, g.Count()))
            .ToList();

        var uncatalogued = map.Items
            .SelectMany(item => item.Uncatalogued.Select(u => (item, u)))
            .GroupBy(x => x.u.Reason ?? "", StringComparer.Ordinal)
            .Select(g => new UncatReason(
                ReasonLabel(map, g.Key),
                g.Sum(x => x.u.Count ?? 0),
                g.Select(x => new UncatRow(Named(x.item), x.u.Count ?? 0, x.u.Note)).ToList()))
            .OrderByDescending(r => r.Calls)
            .ThenBy(r => r.Label, StringComparer.Ordinal)
            .ToList();

        return new View(
            Total: Spec.Operations.Count,
            Recorded: citations.Count,
            Full: rows.Values.Count(r => r.State == OpState.Full),
            Conditional: rows.Values.Count(r => r.State == OpState.Conditional),
            Artifacts: shown.Select(c => c.Item).Distinct().Count(),
            Calls: shown.Count,
            Groups: groups,
            Misses: misses,
            MissedCalls: misses.Sum(m => m.Calls),
            Grades: grades,
            // Said only when the mapping declares an attested grade and nothing
            // here carries it. That is a fact about the evidence worth reading,
            // and it is exactly the fact a page of confident figures hides.
            ObservedTitle: map.ObservedGradeId is { Length: > 0 } observed
                           && !shown.Any(c => map.IsObserved(c.Call.Grade))
                ? map.Grade(observed)?.Title ?? observed
                : null,
            Uncatalogued: uncatalogued,
            UncataloguedCalls: uncatalogued.Sum(r => r.Calls),
            UncataloguedArtifacts: map.Items.Count(i => i.Uncatalogued.Count > 0));
    }

    private static List<Caller> ByArtifact(IReadOnlyList<CoverageCitation> claims) =>
        claims.GroupBy(c => c.Item)
              .Select(g => new Caller(g.Key, g.Select(c => c.Call).ToList()))
              .ToList();

    // ---- resolving a call's operation identity against this spec -----------

    /// <summary>
    /// The operation one identity names, or why this spec has none.
    ///
    /// <para>
    /// This is where the contract's path-matching rule is applied, and it has
    /// to be here rather than in <see cref="CoverageMap"/>: ranking candidates
    /// by how many segments they match literally needs the spec's whole path
    /// set, which the mapping is not and cannot become.
    /// </para>
    ///
    /// <para>
    /// A tie is reported rather than resolved. Two paths matching a call
    /// equally well is the corpus and the mapping failing to determine an
    /// answer between them, and picking one would make this page's figures
    /// depend on which implementation read them.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private (Operation? Op, string? Why) Resolve(OperationIdentity identity, Dictionary<string, List<Operation>> byId)
    {
        if (identity.OperationId is { } id)
            return byId.TryGetValue(id, out var found)
                ? found.Count == 1
                    ? (found[0], null)
                    : (null, $"{found.Count} operations in this spec carry that operationId")
                : (null, "no operation in this spec has that operationId");

        var candidates = Spec.Operations
            .Where(o => string.Equals(o.Method, identity.Method, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
                        && PathMatches(o.Path, identity.Path!))
            .ToList();

        if (candidates.Count == 0)
            return (null, "no path in this spec matches it, at that method and that spelling");

        // Most specific wins: a path spending a literal where another spends a
        // template is the one the caller meant. Without this a catch-all
        // shadows every specific path beside it.
        var best = candidates.Max(o => LiteralSegments(o.Path));
        var top = candidates.Where(o => LiteralSegments(o.Path) == best).ToList();

        return top.Count == 1
            ? (top[0], null)
            : (null, "several paths in this spec match it equally well: "
                     + string.Join(", ", top.Select(o => o.Path).OrderBy(p => p, StringComparer.Ordinal)));
    }

    private static readonly Regex Template = new(@"\{[^{}]*\}");

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether a spec path matches a call path, by the contract's rule: a
    /// templated part of a spec segment matches any run of characters within
    /// one call segment, and everything else matches exactly, INCLUDING case.
    ///
    /// <para>
    /// Both halves are load-bearing and they pull opposite ways. A spec
    /// templating its version where the caller pins one is a spelling
    /// convention, and refusing it reports false gaps by the dozen. A caller
    /// spelling a path segment in the wrong case is a defect in the caller, and
    /// absorbing it would hide a real bug inside the one file whose whole job
    /// is saying what the code does.
    /// </para>
    ///
    /// <para>
    /// Templates are matched WITHIN a segment and not only as whole segments,
    /// because an OData path spells one out as
    /// <c>{entitySetName}({recordId})</c>. Two implementations of the sentence
    /// "a templated segment matches any single literal segment" split on
    /// exactly that, which is how the sentence was found to have two readings.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private static bool PathMatches(string specPath, string callPath)
    {
        var spec = specPath.Split('/');
        var call = callPath.Split('/');
        if (spec.Length != call.Length) return false;

        for (var i = 0; i < spec.Length; i++)
        {
            if (!Template.IsMatch(spec[i]))
            {
                if (!string.Equals(spec[i], call[i], StringComparison.Ordinal)) return false;
            }
            else if (!Regex.IsMatch(call[i], SegmentPattern(spec[i])))
            {
                return false;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }

    private static string SegmentPattern(string segment)
    {
        var pattern = new StringBuilder("^");
        var last = 0;

        foreach (Match match in Template.Matches(segment))
        {
            pattern.Append(Regex.Escape(segment[last..match.Index])).Append("[^/]+");
            last = match.Index + match.Length;
        }

        return pattern.Append(Regex.Escape(segment[last..])).Append('$').ToString();
    }

    /// <summary>How specific a path is: the segments it spells out rather than templates.</summary>
    private static int LiteralSegments(string path) =>
        path.Split('/').Count(segment => !Template.IsMatch(segment));

    // ---- words -------------------------------------------------------------

    private static double Percent(int part, int whole) => whole == 0 ? 0 : part * 100.0 / whole;

    private static string Caveat(GradeTally grade) =>
        grade.Caveat is { Length: > 0 } caveat ? $": {caveat}" : ".";

    private static string MissTitle(int calls) =>
        calls == 1
            ? "1 call names an operation this spec does not carry"
            : $"{calls} calls name operations this spec does not carry";

    /// <summary>
    /// How a state reads on a row.
    ///
    /// <para>
    /// <b>"no recorded caller", never "not called", and the wordiness is the
    /// point.</b> The first two say what a call site in this mapping shows. The
    /// third would be saying that nothing anywhere calls the operation, and
    /// this page has read exactly one mapping, published by one consumer of an
    /// API that may have many. An operation with no row here can perfectly well
    /// have callers nobody has mapped.
    /// </para>
    ///
    /// <para>
    /// With one mapping loaded the two readings look identical on screen, which
    /// is exactly why the overclaiming one is easy to ship and hard to notice.
    /// A page whose whole purpose is making an absence legible is the last
    /// place that should assert more than its data earned. The longer form
    /// naming the mapping is used where the states are introduced and where
    /// they are counted; this short one is for the dense rows.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private static string Words(OpState state) => state switch
    {
        OpState.Full => "called in full",
        OpState.Conditional => "called conditionally",
        _ => "no recorded caller",
    };

    /// <summary>
    /// What one artifact is, in the mapping's own prose, or a neutral word when
    /// it declares none.
    /// </summary>
    private string Kind => Map?.ArtifactKind is { Length: > 0 } kind ? kind : "artifact";

    /// <summary>
    /// The format declares the kind in the singular, which is right: a producer
    /// should not have to spell an English plural for readers it has never met.
    /// The plural is needed in this app and nowhere else, so the naive rule
    /// lives here rather than in the contract.
    /// </summary>
    private string Kinds(int count) => count == 1 ? Kind : Kind + "s";

    private static string Named(CoverageItem item) =>
        item.Name is { Length: > 0 } name ? name : item.Id ?? "an unnamed artifact";

    /// <summary>The declared label for a kind, or null when the mapping declares none.</summary>
    private string? KindLabel(string? kind) =>
        kind is { Length: > 0 } id && Map?.Kinds.TryGetValue(id, out var label) == true ? label : kind;

    /// <summary>
    /// Which entrypoint a call was reached from, in the mapping's declared
    /// words. An undeclared id is shown as written rather than dropped: it is
    /// still what the producer said, and dropping it would leave two calls to
    /// one operation looking like a duplicate row.
    /// </summary>
    private string EntrypointLabel(CoverageCall call) =>
        call.Entrypoint is { Length: > 0 } id
            ? Map?.Entrypoints.TryGetValue(id, out var label) == true ? label : id
            : "no entrypoint declared";

    private static string ReasonLabel(CoverageMap map, string reason) =>
        map.UncataloguedReasons.TryGetValue(reason, out var label) ? label
            : reason.Length > 0 ? reason
            : "no reason declared";

    // ---- navigation --------------------------------------------------------

    // Through Route, never by hand. A hash built from a format string here is a
    // second spelling of the URL contract, and the first thing that goes wrong
    // with one is an id that needed escaping.
    private string OperationHash(Operation op) =>
        new Route(RouteKind.Operation, op.OperationId, SpecId).ToHash();

    private Task Go(Operation op) =>
        OnNavigate.InvokeAsync(new Route(RouteKind.Operation, op.OperationId, SpecId));
}
